@@ -69,6 +69,24 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a JSON string, with syntax highlighting by default. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON, indented by 4 spaces unless you pass a different ``indent``::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+You can also log a JSON renderable directly with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`, by creating a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` object. The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, but default printing still crops long lines to the console width. Enable soft wrapping if you need long lines to be kept whole.
+
+The :func:`rich.print_json` function is also available at the package level, and uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+To pretty print a JSON file from the command line, run the json module as a program::
+
+    python -m rich.json FILE
+
